---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
---

<Base
	title="Loan payoff | Actual Budget Tweaks"
	description="See when a loan or line of credit is paid off and what it costs, in Actual Budget Tweaks."
>
	<main class="docs center">
		<header class="docs__header stack stack--sm">
			<a href="/guides" class="docs__back">Back to guides</a>
			<h1>Loan payoff</h1>
			<p class="docs__lead">
				Loan payoff works out when a loan, mortgage or line of credit is paid off and how much
				interest it costs on the way. Actual keeps the balance; you tell the panel the rate and
				the payment.
			</p>
		</header>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Setting up a loan</h2>
			<p>Turn on Loan payoff in the Features section of Tweaks, then:</p>
			<ol>
				<li>Open an account that owes money and click <strong>Payoff</strong> in its toolbar.</li>
				<li>Enter the annual rate and your monthly payment.</li>
				<li>
					Pick how interest is charged: <strong>Daily</strong> on each day's balance, as most
					lines of credit do, or <strong>Monthly</strong> as a twelfth of the rate, as most fixed
					loans do.
				</li>
				<li>Enter the days of the month the interest posts and the payment lands.</li>
				<li>Click <strong>Save</strong>.</li>
			</ol>
			<p>
				Once saved, the account shows when it will be paid off beside its balance. The figure
				follows the balance as transactions are added.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Trying out changes</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>The chart shows what you owe each month until it reaches zero.</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Scenarios</strong> compares your payment with a lower and a higher one, and says
					how much sooner or later each finishes and what it does to the interest.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Finish by</strong> takes a month and gives the monthly payment that clears the
					debt by then. <strong>Use</strong> puts that payment in the form.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Extra payments</strong> adds one-off payments to the plan. They are for trying
					out and aren't saved.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Nothing here changes the plan beside the balance until you click <strong>Save</strong>.</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Checking the interest</h2>
			<p>
				Enter the payee your lender's interest charges are posted under as the
				<strong>Interest payee</strong>. The panel then uses those charges in two ways:
			</p>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Rate check</strong> works out the rate you were actually charged from recent
					postings and warns when it has moved away from the rate you entered. Variable rates
					change without notice, and this is where it shows.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Next interest</strong> shows the charge to expect on the next interest day. If
					that day has passed and the charge isn't in the account, <strong>Add</strong> enters it
					as an uncleared transaction, which you can undo from the notification.
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Several debts</h2>
			<p>
				When two or more accounts have a saved plan, <strong>All debts</strong> lists when each is
				paid off and the month you are free of all of them.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>Where the settings are kept</h2>
			<p>The settings are saved as one line in the account's note:</p>
			<pre><code>loan: rate=0.0514 daycount=act365 interestDay=20 payment=1860 paymentDay=23 interestPayee=RBC</code></pre>
			<p>
				The rest of the note is left as it is, and scripts using Actual's API can read the same
				line. <strong>Remove</strong> takes the line back out.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section class="docs__section">
			<h2>What it assumes</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>One rate and one payment from today onward, and no new borrowing.</li>
				<li>The payment is the same every month, not a share of the balance.</li>
				<li>Lenders round and count days in their own ways, so expect small differences.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</main>
</Base>
